<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#prince-of-wales">Glossary</a> · Term · title</p><h1 class="enc-title">Prince of Wales</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Prince of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wales">Wales</a> was the title claimed by native Welsh rulers of Gwynedd as overlords of Wales. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/llywelyn-ap-gruffudd">Llywelyn ap Gruffudd</a> won English recognition of it in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-montgomery">Treaty of Montgomery</a> in 1267, but was killed in 1282. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/owain-glyndwr">Owain Glyndŵr</a> revived the title in 1400. Since 1301 it has been given by the English and then British crown to the heir to the throne.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/prince-of-wales.jpg" alt="Prince of Wales" width="320" height="320" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Own work · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Personal_Banner_of_the_Prince_of_Wales.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The first known use of the title Prince of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wales">Wales</a> was in the 1160s, by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/owain-gwynedd">Owain Gwynedd</a>, ruler of Gwynedd, in a letter to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-vii-of-france">Louis VII of France</a>. Owain probably meant to claim pre-eminence over the other Welsh rulers of Deheubarth, Powys and Gwynedd. After his death in 1170 only <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhys-ap-gruffydd">Rhys ap Gruffydd</a> of Deheubarth is known to have used it, in two charters of the 1180s. Gwynedd's rulers preferred styles such as Prince of North Wales, until Dafydd ap Llywelyn revived the title in 1245.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/llywelyn-ap-gruffudd">Llywelyn ap Gruffudd</a>, Dafydd's nephew, used no other style from 1262 until his death in 1282: Prince of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wales">Wales</a> and Lord of Snowdon. By 1263 he had induced the remaining native rulers to do him homage, and he built governing structures that made his authority effective across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/principality-of-wales">Principality of Wales</a>. Henry III of England recognised his title and authority in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-montgomery">Treaty of Montgomery</a> of 1267.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edward-i-of-england">Edward I of England</a> conquered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wales">Wales</a> between 1277 and 1283. Llywelyn was killed in 1282, and his brother Dafydd, who took up his title, was defeated and executed in 1283, after which the principality was permanently annexed. To finalise the conquest, on 7 February 1301 Edward invested his Welsh-born son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edward-ii-of-england">Edward of Caernarfon</a> as Prince of Wales, beginning the custom of granting the title to the heir apparent.</p><p>The title is neither automatic nor heritable. It merges with the Crown when its holder becomes king, and reverts to the Crown if he dies first, leaving the sovereign free to grant it to the new heir. Since 1301 the heir has generally also been made Earl of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chester">Chester</a>, and the prince is typically Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cornwall">Cornwall</a>, a duchy created in 1337 by Edward III for his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edward-the-black-prince">Edward of Woodstock</a>, the Black Prince.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>Two later claimants tried to revive the Welsh title. Owain Lawgoch made an abortive invasion attempt in 1372. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/owain-glyndwr">Owain Glyndŵr</a>'s supporters proclaimed him Prince of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wales">Wales</a> at the start of his revolt in 1400, and in 1404 he had himself crowned and planned state institutions for a new principality. The rebellion began to fail by 1406, and he died in obscurity, probably around 1415. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-vii-of-england">Henry Tudor</a>, the future <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-viii-of-england">Henry VIII</a>, received the title in 1504 after the death of his brother Arthur in 1502, and Charles Stuart, later Charles I, received it in 1616 after the death of his brother Henry Frederick in 1612. Edward, later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edward-viii">Edward VIII</a>, was invested at Caernarfon Castle in July 1911, the first such public investiture for centuries.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Prince of Wales” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Llywelyn ap Gruffudd went further, levying heavy taxes and winning, by the Treaty of Montgomery of 1267, recognition from Henry III as <b>Prince of Wales</b> with the homage of the other Welsh lords.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-gwynedd">Kingdom of Gwynedd · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>For the next decades it was sung with &quot;God Bless the <b>Prince of Wales</b>&quot; and &quot;God Save the Queen&quot; before matches, until sports officials decided in 1975 that it should be sung alone.</blockquote><a href="/songs/hen-wlad-fy-nhadau">Hen Wlad Fy Nhadau</a></li><li><blockquote>It was first performed on 1 August 1740 at Cliveden, the country home of Frederick, <b>Prince of Wales</b>, to mark the accession of his grandfather George I and the birthday of Princess Augusta.</blockquote><a href="/songs/rule-britannia">Rule, Britannia!</a></li><li><blockquote>Wales lost its independence, and in 1301 Edward gave the title <b>Prince of Wales</b> to his own son.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/december-11">11 December in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Edward I invested his son, the future Edward II, with the title <b>Prince of Wales</b> after his conquest of Wales.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/february-7">7 February in history</a></li><li><blockquote>At midnight, at a ceremony attended by the <b>Prince of Wales</b> and President Jiang Zemin, sovereignty passed to China after 156 years of British rule.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/june-30">30 June in history</a></li></ul></section>
